Creating a Flutter app for habit tracking involves several steps, including setting up the project, designing the UI, implementing the logic for tracking habits, and handling data storage. Below is a high-level overview and some code snippets to get you started.

### Step 1: Set Up Your Flutter Project

First, ensure you have Flutter installed. Then, create a new Flutter project:

```bash
flutter create habit_tracker
cd habit_tracker
```

### Step 2: Define Models

Define models for `Habit`, `Group`, and `Task`.

```dart
class Task {
  final String id;
  final String name;
  final int points;

  Task({required this.id, required this.name, required this.points});
}

class Group {
  final String id;
  final String name;
  final List<Task> tasks;

  Group({required this.id, required this.name, this.tasks = const []});
}
```

### Step 3: Create the UI

Create the UI for adding tasks, groups, and displaying summaries.

#### main.dart

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:habit_tracker/screens/home_screen.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      title: 'Habit Tracker',
      theme: ThemeData(
        primarySwatch: Colors.blue,
      ),
      home: HomeScreen(),
    );
  }
}
```

#### home_screen.dart

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class HomeScreen extends StatefulWidget {
  @override
  _HomeScreenState createState() => _HomeScreenState();
}

class _HomeScreenState extends State<HomeScreen> {
  final List<Group> groups = [];

  void addGroup(String name) {
    setState(() {
      groups.add(Group(id: DateTime.now().toString(), name: name));
    });
  }

  void addTask(String groupName, String taskName, int points) {
    final group = groups.firstWhere((g) => g.name == groupName);
    setState(() {
      group.tasks.add(Task(id: DateTime.now().toString(), name: taskName, points: points));
    });
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text('Habit Tracker'),
      ),
      body: Column(
        children: [
          Expanded(
            child: ListView.builder(
              itemCount: groups.length,
              itemBuilder: (context, index) {
                final group = groups[index];
                return Card(
                  child: ListTile(
                    title: Text(group.name),
                    subtitle: Text('Tasks: ${group.tasks.length}'),
                    trailing: Text('Score: ${group.tasks.fold(0, (sum, task) => sum + task.points)}'),
                  ),
                );
              },
            ),
          ),
          ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () {
              showDialog(
                context: context,
                builder: (context) {
                  return AlertDialog(
                    title: Text('Add Group'),
                    content: TextField(
                      onChanged: (value) => addGroup(value),
                      decoration: InputDecoration(hintText: 'Group Name'),
                    ),
                  );
                },
              );
            },
            child: Text('Add Group'),
          ),
        ],
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

### Step 4: Implement Data Storage

For simplicity, we're using in-memory storage. For a production app, consider using a database like SQLite or Firebase.

### Step 5: Add Task Completion Logic

You can add a checkbox or a button to mark tasks as completed and update the score accordingly.

### Step 6: Build and Run

Run the app on both Android and iOS:

```bash
flutter run
```

### Additional Features

- **Persistence**: Use a database to store user data.
- **Notifications**: Implement reminders for tasks.
- **Charts**: Use charts to visualize progress over time.
- **Authentication**: Add user authentication for multiple users.

This is a basic structure to get you started. You can expand on this by adding more features and refining the UI/UX.
